so why hasn't President Obama already  closed Guantanamo I put that question to  Elizabeth beavers she's a security and  human rights advocate at Amnesty  International USA in Washington well  thank you very much for having me and  for covering this really important  development there's been a number of  factors that have prevented Guantanamo  closure from home from over the years  one is a lack of political will from the  Obama administration a closing  Guantanamo is going to take big bold  steps concrete steps towards closure  thankfully this latest transfer shows  that perhaps they do indeed have the  political will to follow through on this  promise to do the right thing and to  close the detention camp but so far they  just haven't exerted that will there are  also a misinformation and false  information fear-mongering that has  crept up in the conversation about  Guantanamo the US public and many  members of the US Congress mistakenly  and don't quite understand exactly who  is at Guantanamo and exactly why it must  be closed and unfortunately that is a  very loud voice it's a very loud  presence in the debate but we still  believe at Amnesty International there  is time if we have the public pressure  and the political will gordana mo can be  closed okay so here's the thing and it  these people many of most of these  people have not been charged they  certainly haven't been tried so if  you're going to ship them out why not  send them back where you got them from  sure you know that the there's a lot of  layers to your question there you're  exactly right there are only 10 people  in Guantanamo who have either been  charged or going through the trial  system that's been set up the Military  Commission system the rest of them are  not facing trial the vast majority are  not even facing charge so you know many  of these individuals were taken from  many different parts of the world  unfortunately some of them are not able  to return to their home countries  because there are security situations or  because they may face human rights  abuses in their home country so many  those who have been transferred must be  resettled elsewhere once detainees are  released from Guantanamo we just heard  about these going back to they got being  sent to the United Arab Emirates having  been released are they free well there's  a lot of diplomatic procedures that goes  into each transfer and there are  agreements that are made separately with  each country that is receiving detainees  at this point we're still collecting  information about these 15 individuals  we have not yet received information  that they are to be further detained but  we're still monitoring the situation you  know it's important to know it's not  enough to just close Guantanamo by  sending people away they must be sent  somewhere safely where their human  rights will be protected
 

